<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/21st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War in Afghanistan (2001–2021)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2001">2001</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> was fought from 2001 to 2021 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and its allies invaded Afghanistan following the attacks of 11 September 2001 and overthrew the Taliban government that sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a>. A long insurgency followed against the new Afghan state and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> forces; after the United States agreed to withdraw, the Taliban took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in August 2021 and restored their regime.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-in-afghanistan-2001-2021.jpg" alt="War in Afghanistan (2001–2021)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pfc. Cameron Boyd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U.S._Army_firefight_in_Kunar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Taliban emerged from religious students who sought to end warlordism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> through stricter adherence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a>. In 1996 they seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, with military support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and financial support from Saudi Arabia, and founded the Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan. They issued edicts forbidding women to work outside the home or attend school. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> reported 15 massacres of civilians between 1996 and 2001, many targeting Shias and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazaras</a>. Under the Taliban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a> trained fighters in Afghanistan, and an estimated 10,000 to 20,000 men passed through the camps before 11 September 2001.</p><p>On that day 19 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a> members hijacked four airliners and carried out coordinated attacks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, in which 2,996 people died, including the hijackers. President George W. Bush demanded that the Taliban hand over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osama-bin-laden">Osama bin Laden</a> and close the camps. The Taliban leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mullah-mohammad-omar">Mullah Omar</a> refused, and the Taliban said bin Laden was protected by Pashtun laws of hospitality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> officially invaded on 7 October 2001 with Operation Enduring Freedom, though CIA teams had entered the country weeks earlier to link up with the Northern Alliance in the Panjshir Valley. With American air support the Northern Alliance took several key cities within weeks, and by November the Taliban had lost most of the country. The coalition formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-security-assistance-force">International Security Assistance Force</a>, and an Afghan Interim Administration was set up. By 2003 the Taliban had reorganised and begun an insurgency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, suicide attacks and reprisals against perceived collaborators.</p><p>Troop numbers grew, with a surge from late 2009 to September 2012. An American covert operation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> killed bin Laden in 2011. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> formally ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-security-assistance-force">ISAF</a> combat operations in 2014 and handed security to the Afghan government, while a new NATO mission, Resolute Support, and the American Operation Freedom's Sentinel continued. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the Taliban concluded a deal for the withdrawal of all American troops. In the summer of that withdrawal the Taliban launched an offensive and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> on 15 August, the day President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashraf-ghani">Ashraf Ghani</a> fled the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The last American military aircraft left on 30 August. The war is estimated to have killed between 176,000 and 212,000 people, including 46,319 civilians, and a further 66,650 people died in the related war in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Although more than 5.7 million former refugees returned after 2001, 2.6 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> were still refugees at the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and about 4 million were internally displaced. It was the longest war in American military history, surpassing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> by seven months.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War in Afghanistan (2001–2021)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
